    "description": "Published studies of Iranian professional football document the use of wearable tracking and statistical analysis to examine match demands and workload. These studies illustrate practical applications of performance analytics while exposing the limits of small samples and injury-prediction models.",
    "tags": ["Sports science", "Performance analysis", "Football", "Wearable technology", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:53:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:53:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Athlete Performance Analytics in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented uses of wearable tracking in Iranian football, from match workload analysis to the limitations of injury prediction."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "measuring-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making physical demands visible",
        "content": "For an athlete, a match result and a physical workload describe different things. Performance analytics helps examine that distinction by turning recorded activity into evidence about the demands of competition. Iranian professional football provides documented examples: researchers have used satellite positioning and accelerometers to study running distance, speed, sprinting and movement-related body load. These measurements describe physical activity; they do not, by themselves, explain tactical quality or individual skill. [s1]\n\nA study published in 2021 examined outfield players from an Iranian top-division team, comparing workloads across match results and between match halves. Its methods describe a practical routine: players wore tracking units, staff downloaded session records, and each player retained an individually assigned unit. Goalkeepers were excluded because their activities differed from those of outfield players. That exclusion matters when interpreting the findings: a result for the sampled players cannot automatically describe an entire squad. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-workload",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Context changes the meaning of a measurement",
        "content": "The same running statistic can arise under different match conditions. The workload study discusses opposition, tactical formation and stoppages as influences on physical demands. Its comparisons therefore offer a way to investigate match context rather than a universal ranking of who performed best. An analyst must preserve the definition of each measure and the circumstances in which it was recorded. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "injury-models",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Prediction remains a research problem",
        "content": "A separate paper published in 2025 used GPS workload records and a decision-tree model to investigate injuries in an Iranian professional team. Its analysis compared recent workload with a longer workload window. Deceleration measures were among the promising signals, but the authors acknowledged a small sample, limited follow-up, scarce injury records and incomplete clinical information. [s2]\n\nThis distinction is essential: a model that separates outcomes within a research sample has not necessarily demonstrated reliable performance for another club or a future season. The paper itself cautions against generalizing its findings across settings. Its results support further investigation, rather than a claim that software can reliably identify which individual will be injured. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-boundaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence establishes",
        "content": "Together, these papers establish that wearable monitoring has been used in Iranian elite football research. They do not establish how widely clubs employ dedicated analysts, what equipment budgets they maintain, or whether monitoring improves results across the league. The useful starting point is the documented measurement process; national adoption and effectiveness remain separate questions requiring broader evidence. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Comparison of Running Distance Variables and Body Load in Competitions Based on Their Results: A Full-Season Study of Professional Soccer Players",
        "publisher": "International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health; University of Verona repository",
        "url": "https://iris.univr.it/retrieve/e14ff6e5-87f9-0209-e053-6605fe0ad24c/ijerph-18-02077-v2.pdf",
        "language": "English",
