    "description": "Iran's digital entertainment includes locally developed games, gaming communities, video sharing and subscription streaming. Its development reflects both creative production and the infrastructure, regulation and cross-border services through which audiences reach that work.",
    "tags": ["Digital culture", "Video games", "Streaming", "Online communities", "Creative industries", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:53:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:53:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Digital Entertainment in Iran | Games, Streaming and Communities",
      "description": "An introduction to Iranian games, developer communities and streaming platforms, with context on internet access and evidence gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "entertainment-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An ecosystem of makers, platforms and audiences",
        "content": "Digital entertainment in Iran connects several kinds of activity. Developers create games; players form communities around them; video services distribute films, series and user uploads. These activities share an internet connection but have different creative processes and business models. An account of the sector therefore needs to distinguish the work being made, the platform carrying it and the audience able to reach it. Developer-association material, game storefronts and audiovisual industry reporting provide evidence for different parts of that picture. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nThe branches below separate computer games, mobile games, esports, studios, competitions and online entertainment platforms. They are useful starting points rather than sealed categories: a studio can work across devices, and a game can be played casually without belonging to an organized competition.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "games-and-skills",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Games as creative production",
        "content": "Iranian game development includes recognizable cultural adaptations. The commercial description of Garshasp: The Monster Slayer presents a fantasy action game drawing on Persian mythology, with combat, exploration and platforming. It illustrates how inherited stories can become interactive entertainment rather than simply being reproduced as written narratives. A storefront establishes a product's credited creators and advertised features; it does not establish the size of its Iranian audience. [s2]\n\nThe International Game Developers Association's Iran chapter describes a complementary layer of activity: education, networking, workshops, game jams and help finding publishers or investors. A game jam is a concentrated collaborative event in which participants develop a game within a limited period. Such activity concerns the skills and relationships behind production, rather than only finished titles. The chapter's page describes its mission; it is not an audited record of how often each activity takes place. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "streaming",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Watching entertainment online",
        "content": "Video-on-demand, usually shortened to VOD, lets audiences select programming rather than follow a fixed broadcast schedule. The Asian Contents & Film Market's Iran report identifies Filimo, Filmnet and Namava as important domestic services. It describes a mixture of subscriptions, advertising and bandwidth-related income, showing why streaming economics differ from cinema ticket sales. [s3]\n\nThe same report connects Filimo and the video-sharing service Aparat through Saba Idea. Their roles should still be distinguished: distributing a catalogue of films and series differs from hosting uploaded videos. The report also acknowledges a lack of transparent figures for platform activity. Its market estimates consequently should not be treated as a consistent statistical series or as a verified count of paying viewers. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-regulation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Availability depends on more than a catalogue",
        "content": "Internet access is part of the entertainment experience. Freedom House's report covering June 2023 through May 2024 documents filtering, connectivity restrictions and policies favoring domestic services. It also describes audiovisual licensing through SATRA, a regulator associated with the state broadcaster. This is a dated account of conditions and regulation, rather than proof that every service remains accessible under the same conditions at the editorial cutoff. [s4]\n\nInternational restrictions require equally careful wording. In guidance updated in May 2024, the United States Treasury's Office of Foreign Assets Control includes qualifying e-gaming services among communications-related authorizations. That guidance does not demonstrate that every game, payment method or commercial relationship is authorized, or that a provider actually serves Iranian users. Legal permission, platform policy and technical reachability are separate questions. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-sector",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the evidence",
        "content": "The inspected sources support a picture of creative production, professional networks and domestic video distribution. They do not support a single total for digital entertainment. A subscription account, a game purchase, a video view and a competition entrant measure different activities. Keeping those distinctions visible makes the sector easier to understand and prevents isolated figures from becoming misleading claims about the whole audience. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
